Choose pages for each statement from the supplied registry.
Include a page when the statement describes that subject's property, action, experience or relationship.
A person's page can include attributes of things they explicitly own; the owned thing can remain without its own page.
For a joint activity or relationship, include every participating subject it describes. A listener is not
necessarily a subject of what they hear.

For each selected page, choose one allowed section and explain what the statement establishes about its subject.
Set owner_entity to the main subject among those pages. Return reason=null because the page explanations suffice.
When no eligible page fits, return pages={}, owner_entity="", and explain why in reason.
Known provisional subjects can receive their first useful page. Preserve the supplied claim IDs.

Select useful reader destinations. A role or responsibility in a project describes both the person and that project; place it on both pages. An incidental object or broad sentiment can stay on the relevant person's or project's page. A new page should provide independent context worth returning to, rather than merely restating one detail.
An unresolved subject has its own neutral registry identity; keep it separate from its possible matches. A useful tentative placement may proceed. Set uncertainty to a short explanation when the selected identity or placement is uncertain, otherwise null. Review is optional; preserve qualifications in the statement.

Choose prominence="briefing" for responsibilities, commitments, dependencies, decisions, constraints, useful working preferences, unresolved questions, and consequential current context. Choose prominence="detail" for routine observations and general sentiments about appreciation or togetherness. An uncertain commitment or a prerequisite can be important to the briefing. Both levels remain searchable and cited.
